@meta
id: 03-08
title: Take back the word you just typed
chapter: 03
type: lesson
skills: insert-ctrl-w
par: 24
minutes: 4
vscode: double-click the word, type over it
open: standup.md

@brief
The refund window went to production yesterday, not to preproduction. Fix
the last word of the Yesterday line.

You have no delete commands yet — chapter 8 gives you those. What you do
have is insert mode, and insert mode has a small set of editing keys of
its own. One of them removes the word behind the cursor in a single press,
which is what Backspace held down thirteen times is trying to be.

@keys
| `A` | append at the end of the line |
| `Backspace` | one character back |
| `Ctrl-W` | delete the word before the cursor |
| `Esc` | out |

@hints
Get to the end of the line in insert mode first — you know the key for
that. Then deal with the wrong word without pressing Backspace fourteen
times.
--
`Ctrl-W` in insert mode deletes the whole word before the cursor. Hold
Ctrl and press w. One press takes `preproduction` with it.
--
`jj` to the Yesterday line, `A`, `Ctrl-W`, type `production`, `Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    j j A <Ctrl-W> production <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Nineteen keystrokes. The Backspace version is twelve extra presses and
you have to watch the screen while you do them, because nothing tells you
when to stop.

`Ctrl-W` is the most useful of the insert-mode editing keys, and it is not
a vim invention — it works in bash, in most terminals, and in a lot of
text fields in a browser. Learn it once, use it everywhere.

Two things it is worth knowing about its idea of a word. It stops at
punctuation, so in `payments.refund.legacy` one press takes `legacy` and
leaves the dot. And it only deletes backwards from the cursor, never
forwards — it is a typing-correction key, not an editing key.

That distinction matters. When you are mid-sentence and get a word wrong,
`Ctrl-W` is right. When you are looking at an existing word in a file you
have not just typed, the right answer is a normal-mode command like `ciw`
in chapter 10, not a trip into insert mode to Backspace over it.

